<!--
     pdf_view — the Sponge OS PDF viewer package (Phase 13 W4, D13.3).

     A source-built mupdf-based viewer (upstream app/pdf_view at
     genode/repos/libports/src/app/pdf_view). The component binary is
     named `pdf_view' (matches the upstream binary and the package
     name, so <binary> matches <name> and the runtime-config generator
     emits a plain <start name="pdf_view"> — no <binary> element needed).

     The metadata <config> below is emitted verbatim into the
     pkg_runtime <start> node by sponge_pkgd's runtime-config generator
     (docs/12 §7.2 rule 4). It carries:

       - libc wiring (stdout/stderr to /dev/log — no pipe/rtc because
         pdf_view never opens the console or a real-time clock; mupdf
         does not need them)
       - a <vfs> with:
           /dev/log           libc plumbing (Genode::log sink)
           /sample.pdf        the bundled sample PDF (mounted from the
                              boot module of the same name; pdf_view's
                              scandir("/", ...) finds it via its .pdf
                              suffix, see main.cc:239-254)

     HOW PDF_VIEW LOCATES THE PDF (the <rom name="..."/> MUST match
     the boot module filename):
         genode/repos/libports/src/app/pdf_view/main.cc:240
           if (scandir("/", &list, pdf_select, alphasort) > 0) {
               char const *file_name = list[0]->d_name;
               int fd = open(file_name, O_BINARY | O_RDONLY, 0666);
               ...
               pdfapp_open(&_pdfapp, (char *)file_name, fd, 0);
     pdf_select (main.cc:62-69) accepts any name ending in ".pdf".
     scandir with alphasort returns the first alphabetical match. The
     boot module of that same name MUST therefore be staged by the run
     scenario (see run/sponge-pdf-view.run) — a mismatch leaves the
     VFS empty and pdf_view exits with "failed to find a PDF to open"
     (main.cc:251).

     Quota (ram="1G" caps="256") matches upstream run/mupdf.run:81
     verbatim — mupdf's font cache and rendered-page buffers are
     memory-hungry on first paint (docs/13 §6 register entry: mupdf
     is the heaviest non-Falkon component in the Alpha app set).

     Only Gui is declared as a session: pdf_view's main.cc opens a
     single Gui::Connection which bundles both framebuffer (Gui) and
     event (Input) sessions (genode/repos/libports/include/gui_session/
     connection.h). The run scenario routes Input explicitly to the
     nitpicker child inside pkg_runtime; the any-service -> any-child
     fallback would also reach nitpicker (which provides both Gui and
     Input), but the explicit route is cheaper and self-documenting.

     No File_system, Report, or Timer session is requested:
     minimum privilege (AGENTS.md §1.2). pdf_view never opens
     files outside its VFS, never emits a report, and never needs a
     timer — the mupdf event loop is driven by Genode's input
     signal handler (main.cc:107-111).
  -->
<package>
  <name>pdf_view</name>
  <version>1.0</version>
  <description>MuPDF-based PDF viewer (renders a single PDF page via the upstream app/pdf_view).</description>

  <binary>pdf_view</binary>
  <quota ram="1G" caps="256"/>

  <launcher category="Utilities"/>

  <dependencies/>

  <config>
    <libc stdout="/dev/log" stderr="/dev/log"/>
    <vfs>
      <dir name="dev"><log/></dir>
      <rom name="sample.pdf"/>
    </vfs>
  </config>

  <sessions>
    <session name="Gui" default-route="parent"/>
  </sessions>
</package>
